WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.617
These six dimensions give us a practical checklist.

00:00:02.617 --> 00:00:06.897
Accuracy asks whether a value reflects reality.

00:00:06.897 --> 00:00:10.012
Completeness asks whether required information is present.

00:00:10.012 --> 00:00:15.397
Consistency asks whether the same concept is represented compatibly.

00:00:15.397 --> 00:00:18.082
Timeliness asks whether the data is current enough.

00:00:18.082 --> 00:00:23.212
Uniqueness asks whether entities appear the intended number of times.

00:00:23.212 --> 00:00:26.842
Validity checks whether values follow documented rules.

00:00:26.842 --> 00:00:33.002
The dimensions can overlap, so treat them as a way to organize investigation rather than as perfectly separate

00:00:33.002 --> 00:00:35.792
boxes. In practice, quality rules should be tied to a use case.

00:00:35.792 --> 00:00:42.382
A fifteen-minute delay may be acceptable for a monthly report but unacceptable for a real-time alert.

00:00:42.382 --> 00:00:51.107
Likewise, an unusual physiological value should often be flagged and investigated rather than automatically

00:00:51.107 --> 00:00:54.080
deleted. Good cleaning preserves evidence and documents uncertainty.
